# Accordion

Stacked disclosure sections on native `<details>`/`<summary>` —
zero JavaScript, single-open by default.

<%= embed :accordion, :playground %>

## When to use

- Long content whose sections are consulted independently: help
  guides, FAQs, policy detail.
- Secondary detail worth keeping on the page but not worth its
  vertical space when closed.

## When not to use

- Parallel views of the same record — `tabs` (see the *Navigation*
  pattern page: sections read in place → accordion; views switched
  between → tabs).
- Content users must see — don't make critical information cost a
  click; put it in the page.
- A single section — one `<details>` is fine, but if it's the whole
  pattern, ask whether the content should just be visible.

## Usage

`render_accordion` (`Gesso::Components::AccordionHelper`) renders the stack of
`<details>` sections.

```erb
<%%= render_accordion(
  id: "faq",
  items: [
    { label: "What is an NHS number?",
      body: "A unique 10-digit number.", open: true },
    { label: "Is my data secure?", body: "Yes — access is logged." }
  ]
) %>
```

| Param | Default | Notes |
| ----- | ------- | ----- |
| `items` | `[]` | `{ label:, body:, open: }` hashes |
| `multiple` | `false` | `true` lets several stay open; default shares a native `name` so the browser keeps one open |
| `id` | `"accordion"` | the shared group name |
| `classes` | — | extra classes on the wrapper |

The chevron rotation on `[open]` and the body animation are CSS
(basecoat + `accordion.css`).

## Do and don't

- Do use `multiple: true` when users genuinely compare sections
  side by side; the single-open default is for one-at-a-time
  reading.
- Do pass `open: true` on the section most users came for, so the
  component never renders fully shut against its content.
- Don't bury actions in accordion bodies — disclosure hides them
  from scanning; actions live at page or card level.
- Don't nest accordions; restructure the content.

## Accessibility and content

- Native `<details>`/`<summary>`: Enter/Space toggles, state is
  announced, in-page find can open sections — platform behaviour,
  no ARIA to maintain.
- Labels are the question or topic, short enough to scan as a list
  — the labels together are the table of contents.
